# Public data handling

## Included

Generic asset aliases, device roles, OS families, connection types, device counts, potential log availability, investigation methods, and bounded findings.

## Omitted

- Real IP addresses and device-to-address mappings.
- All MAC addresses.
- Personal hostnames, owner names, and household relationships.
- Original workbook author fields and saved local folder path.
- Exact authentication-event timestamp and raw browsing/network records.
- Raw captures, event-log exports, command outputs, router exports, and private configuration files.

The public diagram contains no IP or MAC addresses. The inventory is newly authored from approved fields. Standard loopback and wildcard addresses in the command reference explain networking behavior and are not household identifiers.

## Consistency and evidence

Aliases are consistent between the inventory, diagram, and baseline. The private alias mapping is not published. Potential logs do not imply working ingestion; a device's presence does not prove trust. Planned roadmap work is labeled separately from documented observations.

Before adding new evidence, review text, screenshots, document metadata, and embedded files for identifiers and secrets. Keep private originals outside the repository. Ignore rules reduce accidental additions but do not remove data already committed to history. See [GitHub's sensitive-data guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository).
